WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.540
- Thanks to Dashlane for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.970
Have you ever wanted to
know how a lawsuit works?

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.320
Most people think of lawsuits

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.880
as being these big scary animals

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.270
that are completely opaque and esoteric.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.103
But the truth is,

00:00:13.103 --> 00:00:15.690
they are vital to the
functioning of our society

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.110
and can right wrongs where they occur.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.160
But today I wanna demystify the process

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.580
so that if you ever need to use a lawsuit

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.920
or find yourself in the middle of one,

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.043
you'll at least know how they operate.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.570
Hey Legal Eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.400
Today we are covering civil lawsuits

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.180
or as most people refer
to them, just lawsuits.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.060
As always, be sure to
subscribe and comment

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.450
in the form of an objection,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.310
which I will either sustain or overrule.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.640
I wanted to cover lawsuits today

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.620
because there are a lot of misconceptions

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.270
about how the civil process works.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.650
And let me start out this discussion

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:58.010
by drawing a distinction
between the civil process of law

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.500
and the criminal process.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.130
The criminal process is probably the one

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.000
that you see most often
on TV and in movies.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.490
That is where you have committed a crime

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.860
and you are prosecuted by the state.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.610
And generally the penalties
for a criminal offense

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.960
is jail time.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.940
Sometimes there are monetary penalties

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.230
that go along with a crime

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.500
but the bottom line is
that generally speaking

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.090
the criminal process involves the state

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.120
prosecuting you for
crimes against society.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.290
The civil process on the
other hand is different.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.270
The civil process is
how you get compensation

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.210
for damages that are
caused by another person.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.530
Damages that are caused
in the form of usually

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.060
a breach of contract
or what we call torts,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.770
which are civil wrongs,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.460
things that are not crimes

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.160
but are violations of norms such

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.230
that we believe that one person

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:48.580
should compensate the other.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.360
Generally speaking, the end
goal of the civil process

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.030
is to receive monetary compensation

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.760
for the wrongs that you have suffered.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.420
As opposed to the criminal process

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.080
where you have done a
wrong against society

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.280
and you can go to jail as a result.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.500
So today we're going to be
talking exclusively about

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.420
the civil law process,
not the criminal process

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.420
and the main tool of the civil process

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.880
which is of course is the lawsuit.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.720
Now of course, there are
always a million exceptions

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.290
to the things I'm going to talk about

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.800
which is why there are lawyers

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.920
and you should always consult a lawyer

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.440
before engaging in any
kind of civil process.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.490
But today I wanna go
over the broad strokes

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.680
so you have a general idea
of how a lawsuit works.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.060
A lawsuit starts with
what's called a complaint.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.450
A complaint is the document
that initiates your civil suit

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.960
against somebody else.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.190
It require a couple of things.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.330
Primarily it is your factual
statement of allegations

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.200
as to what happened.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.680
How you have been wronged

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.220
and why you are entitled to
some sort of compensation.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.880
It contains a statement of
proper jurisdiction and venue

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.140
to show that this
particular court is the one

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.460
that should be hearing
this particular lawsuit.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.920
But for the most part it contains
your factual allegations.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.530
Your story of what happened.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.620
And at the end of the complaint

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.040
there Is this statement of law

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.100
that explains the causes of action

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.010
that give rise to your particular claim.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.720
So after you have the factual statement

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:11.840
about what happened,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.020
you'll have the legal statement

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.020
about why those particular facts

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.300
fit the elements of a particular
cause of action or claim

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.910
and that entitles you to
some sort of compensation.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.530
So for example, a few weeks back I covered

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.900
the Fornite dance controversy.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.430
Where certain artists were
suing video game manufacturers,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.620
claiming that they had stolen
their copyrighted dance.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.130
In their complaints,

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.320
they laid out that
these particular artists

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.590
had in fact created a copyrightable works

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.050
in the form of dances.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.970
So that was the factual predicate.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.450
They laid out that the
video game manufacturers

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.750
had taken these copyrighted works,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.320
used them without permission,

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:52.800
again the factual predicate,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.700
and then at the end they stated

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.080
that this was a violation
of copyright law,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.900
citing to the federal standard
for copyright infringement.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.020
So that's an example what
the complaint looks like,

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.470
it's the initiating document,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.970
it contains the factual
and legal reasoning

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.130
for why you think you
should get compensation

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.820
from another person.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.300
The next step after
someone files a complaint,

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.630
generally speaking, is
to file what's called

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.330
an answer or a motion to dismiss.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.170
The person who files the complaint

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:21.830
is called the plaintiff.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:22.850
And generally speaking,

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.510
the person who responds to the
complaint is the defendant.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.280
Now once the plaintiff
files the complaint,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.860
the defendant has basically two options.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.770
One is called an answer,

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.050
the other is called a motion to dismiss.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.480
The answer, not surprisingly,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.210
is an answering document to the complaint.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.550
Which goes line by line
over the factual allegations

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.150
in the complaint and either admits

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.700
to those particular facts or denies them.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.040
Now it might seem strange to
admit anything in a complaint

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.070
but really in any given lawsuit

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.480
there will be things
that both sides agree to,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.870
facts that are not really in dispute

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:01.000
and facts that they deny and
that they say never happened.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.130
So the purpose of the answer
is to stipulate the facts

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.200
that are not in dispute
and to deny the facts

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.560
that are in dispute.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.650
The second option, if it is available,

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.530
is called a motion to dismiss.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.600
A motion to dismiss
doesn't challenge the facts

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.020
of the complaint,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.710
there will be time for
that later in the lawsuit.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.320
The motion to dismiss simply says

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.340
that even if everything
that the plaintiff says

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.460
is 100% true from a factual standpoint,

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:29.990
it still doesn't give rise
to any kind of legal remedy.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:31.970
It is legally invalid.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.190
So let's say that someone
files a lawsuit against you

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.400
for being a big mean jerk face.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.710
Well there's no cause of
action called jerk face.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.120
The law doesn't allow
you to get compensation

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.510
just because someone
was a jerk face to you.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.010
That might make you a bad person

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.430
but that doesn't mean that you have

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.300
an obligation to pay
them any sort of money.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.880
However, let's say that
someone files a claim

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.780
for defamation.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.480
Defamation requires one, a
false statement of a fact

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.780
rather than an opinion,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.223
publication or communication
of that statement,

00:06:03.223 --> 00:06:05.870
and fault amounting to at least negligence

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.700
and then suffering damages as a result

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.140
of that untrue fact statement.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.700
Now let's assume that the
complaint alleging defamation

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.310
is 100% factually incorrect.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.970
You either never said
the statement in question

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.382
or the statements are true and therefor

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:26.210
couldn't have been a false
fact that was communicated.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:29.480
In that circumstance a motion
to dismiss wouldn't work.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:30.620
Because a motion to dismiss

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.680
assumes that the facts in
the complaint are true.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.800
A motion to dismiss only
tests the legal sufficiency

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:38.950
of the complaint.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.330
And in this particular case,

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.810
someone has alleged legal
predicate for defamation.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.270
There will be time later on to
show that the facts are wrong

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:48.400
but effectively at these early stages

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.470
the only things that you
can do as a defendant

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:53.480
are either answer or
file a motion to dismiss.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.180
Which only tests the legal
sufficiency of the complaint

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.580
not the factual sufficiency.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.610
And if you lose a motion to dismiss

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.370
then you will just have to answer

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.180
just like any other defendant.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.370
So once the complaint and
answer have been filed

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.160
the court has said that
at least as alleged

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.840
there is legal sufficiency
for the lawsuit,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.220
then what generally happens is

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.470
you proceed into what's called discovery.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.820
Discovery is the process
by which both sides

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.340
give up all the information

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.820
that is relevant to
the particular lawsuit.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.230
Both the claims that
the plaintiff is making

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.700
and any defenses that the defendant has.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.830
Long gone are the days where
you can ambush people at trial.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:35.810
Trial is really just the
tip of the lawsuit iceberg.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:39.020
For the most part, most of
the lawsuit consists of this

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.930
factual discovery back and
forth because we've decided

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.870
that it is better for the judicial process

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.520
for people to have all of
the relevant information

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.860
that is necessary to
adjudicate these cases

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.550
before going to trial.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:54.170
Discovery consists of essentially

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.220
requests from one side to the other

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.030
and then responses from that side

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.520
back to the original requesting side.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.830
This includes things like interrogatories

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.530
which are questions that the
other side has to answer.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:08.450
It includes document requests

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:12.020
where you request a certain
category of documents.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:13.630
And unless it's objectionable,

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:15.070
the other side must respond

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:17.830
by providing all of those documents.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.950
You'll often see on TV and movies,

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.500
people talking about
being buried in documents.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:25.010
One way of responding to document requests

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:28.560
is to not look at them
discreetly and specifically

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.470
and just giving the
other side every document

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.400
that could be responsive to the request

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.880
that you have made.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.200
So it's often in your best interest

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:40.630
to write your document
request in such a way

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.860
that you get the documents that you want

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.430
but don't create this really huge bourdon

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:46.360
from the other side

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.205
to compile hundreds of thousands
or millions of documents

00:08:50.205 --> 00:08:51.830
as a result of these requests.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.750
You need to thread that needle.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.700
And of course discovery
also consists of depositions

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.370
which is where you get
to question a witness.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:01.930
Witnesses are under oath,

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.950
they're responses are
recorded by a stenographer.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:08.050
Recently I just reviewed
the movie The Social Network

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:09.364
which is really interesting
from my perspective

00:09:09.364 --> 00:09:12.150
because it is told almost entirely

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.010
through deposition testimony.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.680
And you can imagine that
when you're in a lawsuit

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.030
being able to question witnesses

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:20.880
and parties to that lawsuit

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.550
can be a very, very important tool

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.620
to find out what happened

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:28.220
in any particular legal situation.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:30.560
It's a great way to get
evidence and to be able to

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.330
destroy the other side's
legal claims as well.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:37.240
But of course the downside to
the factual discovery process

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.040
is that it is very resource intensive.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:43.780
It takes a ton of time to draft requests,

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.380
to respond to requests.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.730
And then of course to cull through

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.340
sometimes millions of pages of documents

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.270
just to look for the things
that support your case

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.820
or support your defense.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.560
Discovery is what makes
up the vast majority

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.560
of litigating a lawsuit.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:00.570
So generally speaking,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:04.520
once factual discovery has
commenced or has completed,

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.240
one or both of the parties
will file what's called a

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.320
motion for summary judgment.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:12.310
Now where a motion to dismiss

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.390
does not attach any evidence whatsoever

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:17.610
because it takes the allegations
and the complaint as true,

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:21.280
a motion for summary
judgment can use evidence.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.180
You will compile some of the evidence

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.580
that you gathered through
document requests,

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.850
through interrogatories
and through depositions,

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.340
you will attach that to your motion

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.540
and it's basically a trial on paper.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:33.890
The motion for summary judgement

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:38.530
is a way of ending a lawsuit
early without getting to trial.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:41.070
But the downside is that both sides

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:45.460
effectively must agree to the
evidence that is undisputed.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.960
And of course many
sides do in fact dispute

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:50.840
the evidence at the motion
for summary judgment stage.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.860
But a judge will take
that into consideration

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.720
and will look at the evidence presented

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.490
and actually determine if the parties

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.950
really do dispute the evidence

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:01.470
or if the other side can
offer contradictory evidence.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.600
So assuming that it's
the defendant that files

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:04.990
a motion for summary judgment

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:08.940
and says that even with undisputed
facts the lawsuit fails,

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.860
the plaintiff can respond to
a motion for summary judgment

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:15.570
by saying legally their
case is still sound

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:17.810
and that even with these undisputed facts

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:19.430
they are entitled to a legal remedy.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.410
Or the plaintiff can beat a
motion for summary judgment

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:24.200
by demonstrating that in fact

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.050
the facts go different ways,

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.600
that there's contradictory evidence

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.050
and that the facts are disputed

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:30.930
in such a way that a
motion for summary judgment

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.150
should not be granted.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:33.870
Now assuming that all motions

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.030
for summary judgment are denied,

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.470
that generally leads to pretrial motions

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.000
and what we call motions in limine

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.230
which are motions that deal
with evidentiary issues.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.250
One thing you see in legal movies

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:47.630
and legal TV shows all the time

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.940
is that they have all of
these evidentiary disputes

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.720
in the middle of trial.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.950
That's not particularly realistic.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.930
For the most part, if you have evidence

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:59.460
that A, you think is
really, really important

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.640
and B, you think the other
side is going to contest

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.280
and not stipulate to the
admission of that evidence,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.950
what you'll want to do
is file a pretrial motion

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.660
to allow that evidence to come in.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.420
So for the most part, all
of the really important

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.710
evidentiary issues don't
happen in the middle of trial

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:19.970
where there's this Perry Mason moment

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.340
where you're not sure if the judge

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.510
is going to allow your
evidence to come in or not.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.202
That's taken care of
months before the trial

00:12:26.202 --> 00:12:28.820
in pretrial motions and motions in limine

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.550
because if the evidence
is really important

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:31.920
and you need it to come in,

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:33.580
you want to provide as much briefing

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:34.780
and as much legal reasoning

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:36.180
as to why it should come in.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.060
Or legal reasoning as to why
the other side's evidence

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.110
should be kept out

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.490
because you'll never be able to do as much

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.410
in oral argument especially
on the day of trial.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.750
After the pretrial issues are dealt with

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.040
that leads to trial.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:52.330
Now less than 2% of all filed lawsuits

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.490
ever get anywhere close to trial.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:55.420
For the most part,

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:59.180
most lawsuits are either
rejected at the motion stage

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:00.950
or they will settle ahead of time

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.040
long before you get to trial.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.920
Because trial is incredibly expensive.

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.920
But assuming that you get to trial,

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.250
of course everybody knows
about opening statements

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.350
where each side will explain

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.560
what facts they intend to bring out

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.280
in that particular trial.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.330
Then you go on to examinations

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:19.580
where you conduct the direct examination

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:21.800
of your witnesses, the
plaintiff goes first.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.300
Then each individual
witness is cross-examined

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.990
by the defense.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:27.750
Then the defense witnesses go

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:30.730
where the defendants will
provide their witnesses

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:34.300
who are examined and
cross-examined by the plaintiffs.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:35.710
Then there are closing arguments

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:38.430
which is the one place
where you are allowed

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.750
to engage in argument.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.850
Openings are generally
supposed to be argument free,

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:46.411
just explaining what kind
of facts are gonna come out.

00:13:46.411 --> 00:13:48.310
Closing arguments is where you are able

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.130
to tie everything together.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.480
And then after closing
argument the trier of fact,

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:55.000
whether that is a judge or a jury,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.840
will render a decision about
which side wins or loses.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:00.590
There can be an appeal.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.730
On appeal, you're not retrying the facts,

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.220
the facts are set by the trial court.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.600
On appeal the only
things that are at issue

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.550
are discreet legal issues.

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:12.160
With a given set of
facts the court of appeal

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:15.310
will look at the decisions
that the trial court made

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.740
and determine if they were legally correct

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.000
based on those facts.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.450
Of course there are exceptions to this

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:22.410
but that's generally what's
happening when you appeal.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.730
And by the way, I've heard
so many times people say,

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:28.110
I'm goin to appeal this all
the way to the Supreme Court.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:29.380
No, no you're not.

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.930
The Supreme Court doesn't really review

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:33.600
run of the mill lawsuits.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.350
The Supreme Court only takes a case

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.270
if there is a tricky
Constitutional question

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.730
or a question of first impression

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:41.810
that deals with federal laws.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:45.200
Odds are, your lawsuit
doesn't come anywhere close

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:46.360
to those issues,

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:49.160
so there's no way you
will ever appeal your case

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:49.993
to the Supreme Court.

00:14:49.993 --> 00:14:51.900
It's just not going to happen.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:53.990
So that's how a basic civil lawsuit works.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:57.230
